      ":question/id": "b11418bd-889d-526f-a552-78bc2595e9b7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Rewrite $\\frac{225}{4}-15z+z^2$ in the form $(z+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put the quadratic term first: $\\frac{225}{4}-15z+z^2=z^2-15z+\\frac{225}{4}$.\n\nThe half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^2-15z+\\frac{225}{4}&=\\underbrace{z^2-2\\cdot \\frac{15}{2}z+\\frac{225}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{225}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{225}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-15z$.\n\nComparing with $(z+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{15}{2}$ and $b=0$.",
